data.content:
# Migrating `BackgroundProcessor` to .NET 8

## Key Problem

`Thread.Abort()` and `Thread.ResetAbort()` throw `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 8. The entire cancellation model must change.

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Task _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token); // pass token to unblock
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts == null || _workerTask == null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait with timeout instead of Thread.Sleep + Abort
        var effectiveTimeout = timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
        if (await Task.WhenAny(_workerTask, Task.Delay(effectiveTimeout)) != _workerTask)
        {
            // Log warning: worker didn't stop in time
            // In .NET 8 there is NO way to forcibly kill the thread
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts?.Cancel();
        _workerTask?.Wait(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Critical Pitfalls

| Issue | Detail |
|-------|--------|
| **`Thread.Abort()` throws at runtime** | It compiles fine but throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`. No compile-time warning. |
| **`Thread.ResetAbort()` gone** | Same — `PlatformNotSupportedException`. |
| **Blocking calls ignore cancellation** | If `GetNextItem()` blocks on a synchronous wait (e.g., `BlockingCollection.Take()`), cancellation won't interrupt it. You must use the overload that accepts a `CancellationToken`. |
| **No forcible thread kill exists** | If code is truly stuck (deadlock, infinite loop in third-party code), .NET 8 has no equivalent to `Abort()`. The only escape hatch is `Environment.Exit()` or process-level isolation. |
| **`volatile bool` is insufficient** | A volatile flag can't unblock a thread waiting inside a kernel call. `CancellationToken` integrates with async I/O and wait handles. |

## Migration Checklist

1. **Replace `Thread` with `Task.Run` or `BackgroundService`** (if hosted in ASP.NET Core / Generic Host).
2. **Replace `volatile bool` with `CancellationTokenSource`**.
3. **Thread all blocking calls with `CancellationToken`** — this is the hard part. Audit every call inside the loop.
4. **Replace `catch (ThreadAbortException)` with `catch (OperationCanceledException)`**.
5. **Replace `Thread.Sleep` with `Task.Delay`** (or `await token.WaitHandle.WaitOneAsync()`).
6. **If using Generic Host**, consider `BackgroundService` which handles the lifecycle for you:

```csharp
public class ProcessorService : BackgroundService
{
    protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken stoppingToken)
    {
        while (!stoppingToken.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            var item = await GetNextItemAsync(stoppingToken);
            await ProcessItemAsync(item, stoppingToken);
        }
    }
}
```

## If You Can't Make Blocking Code Async

For legacy synchronous APIs that don't accept a `CancellationToken`:

```csharp
// Poll with short timeouts instead of one long block
private object GetNextItemWithCancellation(CancellationToken token)
{
    while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        if (_queue.TryTake(out var item, TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(500)))
            return item;
    }
    throw new OperationCanceledException(token);
}
```

This replaces the "abort as a last resort" pattern with cooperative polling — the only safe option in .NET 8.
